      "role": "user",
      "content": "This is perfect. It really highlights how \"professionalism\" is often used as a proxy for social class and race. \n\nTo make this workshop even more impactful, I want to dive into the \"logic\" behind these biases. I want to create a section titled \"The Cognitive Shortcuts of Bias\" to help leaders understand *why* their brains make these connections so quickly.\n\nCan you expand on these three profiles? Specifically, for each one, can you explain the \"mental archetype\" the manager is using? For example, if the manager thinks the AAVE speaker \"sounds too street,\" what is the specific social archetype or stereotype they are subconsciously projecting onto that person (e.g., associating certain speech with criminality, lower education, or instability)? \n\nI want to show that these aren't just random thoughts, but are driven by deep-seated, specific stereotypes about what these different groups \"actually\" represent in society.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
